WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.420
Every Bitcoin you buy today is probably worth $13 million in US dollars in 21 years.

2
00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:08.600
I'd love to tell you I understood all this four years ago.

3
00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:14.020
If I boil down everything you learn in business school to one sentence, it's keep.

4
00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:17.580
Do you get nervous when Bitcoin price drops?

5
00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:21.280
Maybe you're more bearish.

6
00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:25.000
The bear case would be only $3 million a coin, and the bull case is...

7
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:33.000
The way I made money in big tech investments is I find something everybody needs, nobody can stop, and most people...

8
00:00:33.940 --> 00:00:40.480
Diversification doesn't make sense if your life depends upon making the one right choice.

9
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:44.280
Are you repelling money? Are you scaring it away?

10
00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:47.040
Or are you attracting it?

11
00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:50.300
But, Bonnie, that will make you interesting.

12
00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:57.520
Interesting means there's this point where you're either going to have a fast death or slow death or take a risk.

13
00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:01.220
Taiwan Semiconductor, it does something that nobody understood.

14
00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:04.600
What's really going on here is a revolution in thinking.

15
00:01:05.240 --> 00:01:07.300
The way you make a lot of money, Bonnie, is...

16
00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:25.340
Hey, nice to meet you, Bonnie.

17
00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:36.020
As I was preparing for this conversation, I came across some really interesting things about you online.

18
00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:38.540
And I want to ask you how accurate this is, okay?

19
00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:42.480
So I read that you spend your childhood living in different countries around the world,

20
00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:48.120
and that you enjoy comic books, but, you know, your parents encourage you to read regular books

21
00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:51.860
by offering you a dime for each book you finish.

22
00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:54.420
And that sparked your interest in reading.

23
00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:57.300
And I want to know how accurate these stories are.

24
00:01:58.440 --> 00:01:59.980
Yeah, that's a true story.

25
00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:04.500
Yeah, so I grew up in an Air Force, on Air Force bases.

26
00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:06.920
My father is in the Air Force his entire career.

27
00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:14.800
And we lived in Japan, and we lived in New Zealand, and we lived all through the West,

28
00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:18.200
and in Florida and Virginia.

29
00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:20.900
So that was a nice upbringing.

30
00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:27.720
But I remember that when I was around first grade, I had this serious love of comic books.

31
00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:33.420
And I think they cost 25 cents at the time.

32
00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:36.140
And I couldn't afford them.

33
00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:39.460
But my first grade teacher had a reading competition.

34
00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:43.660
And, you know, there was competition to see how many books you could read.

35
00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:49.120
And my parents told me they would give me one dime, 10 cents per book.

36
00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:53.120
And I was very competitive at the time.

37
00:02:54.180 --> 00:03:00.020
Probably just, I was really into Silver Surfer or, you know, Fantastic Four comic books.

38
00:03:00.020 --> 00:03:06.120
So I discovered I could go to the library, and I could check out seven books at a time.

39
00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:11.120
And so I was looking for books, and I checked out a bunch of science fiction books.

40
00:03:12.280 --> 00:03:15.040
And so I get seven of them, and I read them all.

41
00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:22.200
And then I would get the 70 cents, and then I would go back and get seven more, and I'd read them.

42
00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:28.060
And then as fast as I read the books, I would be buying comic books.

43
00:03:28.940 --> 00:03:36.360
And I think that summer, I read some, you know, ridiculous number of books.

44
00:03:36.580 --> 00:03:39.920
Like normally one book a day for the entire summer.

45
00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:44.580
But something like 70 or 80 books during the summer of this competition.

46
00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:47.200
And I did it primarily to fund my comic book habit.

47
00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:51.300
But by the time I finished the summer, I actually enjoyed reading.

48
00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:57.740
I mean, you know, and I got really into science fiction.

49
00:03:57.740 --> 00:04:09.640
And I discovered Robert Heinlein and Arthur C. Clarke and Isaac Asimov and many of the great inspirations that defined the rest of my life.

50
00:04:09.820 --> 00:04:15.940
And I'm sure by the time I was in third grade, I'd read all of those works.

51
00:04:15.940 --> 00:04:18.020
And then I proceeded to reread them.

52
00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:21.220
And I think that really accelerated my intellectual development.

53
00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:24.080
So thank you to my parents for that little trick.

54
00:04:24.260 --> 00:04:24.860
It worked.

55
00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:25.620
That's amazing.

56
00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:27.740
Do you still read comic books at all?

57
00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:30.360
Not so much anymore.

58
00:04:33.820 --> 00:04:34.260
Okay.

59
00:04:35.020 --> 00:04:40.700
You know, Taiwanese people, we love to invest in U.S. stock, Taiwanese stock.

60
00:04:40.700 --> 00:04:46.660
And I want to ask you about MicroStrategy before that, just to make sure I have all the numbers right.

61
00:04:47.140 --> 00:04:52.340
MicroStrategy currently holds 252,220 Bitcoins.

62
00:04:52.600 --> 00:04:53.120
That's correct.

63
00:04:53.300 --> 00:05:02.560
And based on what you announced four years ago, you personally hold 17,732 Bitcoins.

64
00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:03.660
Is that correct?

65
00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:05.920
A bit more than that now.

66
00:05:06.280 --> 00:05:08.500
But that's what I announced four years ago.

67
00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:09.020
Yes.

68
00:05:09.980 --> 00:05:10.380
Okay.

69
00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:16.320
You know, when it comes to finance, we've been taught to diversify.

70
00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:21.080
But that doesn't seem to be the strategy, MicroStrategy or you have followed.

71
00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:25.700
What inspired you to go this route?

72
00:05:25.700 --> 00:05:31.000
And how did you initially convince the board and the investors to be on board with it?

73
00:05:32.840 --> 00:05:46.620
Yeah, I think, first of all, the idea of diversification, it makes sense if you have an array of options, all of them risky and most of them poorly understood.

74
00:05:46.620 --> 00:06:06.080
And so if I give you 500 companies and it takes you 100 hours to understand each company and you don't have 100 hours times 500 to understand the companies, then simply buying the entire portfolio of all 500 of them is not an irrational idea.

75
00:06:06.900 --> 00:06:14.340
But diversification doesn't make sense if your life depends upon making the one right choice.

76
00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:37.300
So if you're on a sinking ship and there are 10 lifeboats and one of them is functional and in the water and floating and the other nine have a hole in them and you can see that they have a hole in them and they're not going to get deployed, then simply putting the 10 kids, each one of them in a different boat, isn't a wise thing.

77
00:06:37.380 --> 00:06:40.180
It's better to put all your family into the boat that's floating.

78
00:06:40.180 --> 00:06:47.220
And often it's the same with a car or any kind of technology you understand.

79
00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:51.400
And many times you can't even afford, right?

80
00:06:51.420 --> 00:06:53.280
Like the city can have one bridge.

81
00:06:54.040 --> 00:06:56.420
The city can't afford 10 bridges.

82
00:06:56.840 --> 00:07:01.660
It can't afford to diversify and have 10 bridges over the same river.

83
00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:03.540
It doesn't make things better.

84
00:07:03.940 --> 00:07:06.200
It will make the city bankrupt.

85
00:07:06.200 --> 00:07:09.820
And so sometimes you just have to come to the right choice.

86
00:07:09.820 --> 00:07:26.360
And the last point is, if you're in an economy with hyperinflation and the currency is going to zero and everybody's going to starve to death, then choosing to leave half your family in the country and take the other half out of the country because you want to be diversified is just foolish, right?

87
00:07:26.500 --> 00:07:31.160
If your house is on fire, you should take the entire family out of the house.

88
00:07:31.160 --> 00:07:39.200
You shouldn't leave half the kids in the house and half the kids in the backyard outside the house in order to be safe, right?

89
00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:41.760
You're not safer if there's a problem.

90
00:07:42.440 --> 00:07:42.520
Yeah.

91
00:07:42.600 --> 00:07:54.880
You know, so the trope of diversification is appropriate when you don't know the right answer and you're placing a bunch of bets across a bunch of different alternatives.

92
00:07:54.880 --> 00:08:02.720
But when you actually have math and physics and common sense to guide you, then nobody diversifies.

93
00:08:03.240 --> 00:08:07.060
You know, you choose the best outcome and you do that.

94
00:08:08.100 --> 00:08:11.480
So why MicroStrategy discovered Bitcoin?

95
00:08:11.660 --> 00:08:13.760
Well, we had $500 million of cash.

96
00:08:13.820 --> 00:08:15.600
It was generating 0% interest.

97
00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:28.640
The cost of capital was 15% to 20% and we were basically looking at a situation where the company was going to lose as much money holding the cash as it made through its operations each year.

98
00:08:29.260 --> 00:08:31.120
And therefore, we were failing as a business.

99
00:08:31.400 --> 00:08:32.640
The company was going to die.

100
00:08:33.300 --> 00:08:36.700
And so we had to find something to do with the money.

101
00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:40.600
How do you invest the money in something that will return more than the cost of capital?

102
00:08:40.600 --> 00:08:45.820
The cost of capital and the traditional dollar economy is the S&P return.

103
00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:48.120
It's maybe 12% to 14% a year.

104
00:08:49.260 --> 00:08:53.600
If you're returning 0%, you're losing 12% to 14% of your capital a year.

105
00:08:53.760 --> 00:09:01.420
And if you're returning 24%, you know, you're adding 24% nominally or 12% in real terms every single year.

106
00:09:01.420 --> 00:09:11.040
So Bitcoin turns out to be the only commodity in the world that is actually a scarcity.

107
00:09:11.300 --> 00:09:14.520
It's the only commodity that's a good investment over the long period.

108
00:09:15.080 --> 00:09:19.400
Every other commodity will underperform the S&P index because they're not scarce.

109
00:09:20.040 --> 00:09:23.960
Bitcoin will overperform the S&P index because it is scarce.

110
00:09:23.960 --> 00:09:28.780
And so it's unique as the only commodity that actually is good investment.

111
00:09:29.000 --> 00:09:33.260
And it's also the only digital commodity, an asset without an issuer.

112
00:09:33.680 --> 00:09:38.320
And that gives it regulatory superiority for a publicly traded company.

113
00:09:39.280 --> 00:09:45.260
Because public companies in the U.S. can't hold more than 40% of their balance sheet in a security.

114
00:09:45.500 --> 00:09:48.260
They have to own property or a commodity.

115
00:09:48.260 --> 00:09:56.720
So MicroStrategy was, you know, facing a quick death or a slow death or innovate, try a new idea.

116
00:09:57.060 --> 00:09:59.720
And so we tried the new idea.

117
00:10:00.080 --> 00:10:04.300
And that's what got us on the path to becoming a Bitcoin company.

118
00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:12.680
Was it difficult to convince people, though, because 2020 people were still arguing if Bitcoin was a scam?

119
00:10:13.040 --> 00:10:15.260
Yeah, it was a very methodic process.

120
00:10:15.260 --> 00:10:17.860
First, I had to convince myself.

121
00:10:18.480 --> 00:10:20.680
In 2013, I thought it was going to fail.

122
00:10:21.880 --> 00:10:24.880
What we say is that everybody's against Bitcoin before they're for it.

123
00:10:25.700 --> 00:10:27.860
And Bitcoin's on a need-to-know basis.

124
00:10:28.160 --> 00:10:36.120
So if you don't need it, if your life didn't depend on it, you would dismiss it as a nuisance or an oddity of some sort.

125
00:10:36.360 --> 00:10:40.060
It's like electricity or a new thing like a motor.

126
00:10:40.060 --> 00:10:48.520
However, if you didn't expect to find clean, silent power, then you wouldn't know you need to redesign your factory in 1700 for electricity.

127
00:10:48.720 --> 00:10:49.920
You wouldn't look for it.

128
00:10:50.080 --> 00:10:58.320
If you had a factory in 1900 and you're going out of business because your competitor is producing faster and cheaper than you,

129
00:10:58.320 --> 00:11:05.280
and your choice was to go out of business, go bankrupt, and lose everything and be on the street, or check out the new electricity thing,

130
00:11:06.060 --> 00:11:08.380
you would go and check out the new electricity thing.

131
00:11:08.440 --> 00:11:10.100
You had everything to gain, nothing to lose.

132
00:11:10.620 --> 00:11:13.200
And that's how paradigm shifts take place.

133
00:11:13.260 --> 00:11:14.940
And that's how innovation takes place.

134
00:11:15.500 --> 00:11:18.980
Throughout human history, Bitcoin is digital energy.

135
00:11:18.980 --> 00:11:26.820
People are afraid of fire until they realize that fire might be a good thing, or they're afraid of electricity until they realize electricity is a good thing.

136
00:11:26.980 --> 00:11:29.220
So we start by convincing ourself.

137
00:11:29.700 --> 00:11:32.320
And then after that, educating the management team.

138
00:11:32.700 --> 00:11:35.000
And after that, educating the board of directors.

139
00:11:35.200 --> 00:11:37.940
And after that, educating the auditors and the accountants.

140
00:11:38.280 --> 00:11:40.340
And after that, educating the shareholders.

141
00:11:40.340 --> 00:11:49.040
And after that, progressively upgrading the company and evolving and finding new sets of shareholders.

142
00:11:49.640 --> 00:11:52.220
And so it really is an evolutionary process.

143
00:11:52.500 --> 00:11:56.240
It's like concentric circles of consensus.

144
00:11:57.160 --> 00:12:03.800
And each of them requires hours or tens of hours of contemplation and dialogue.

145
00:12:04.160 --> 00:12:07.100
How long did it take for you to convince yourself?

146
00:12:07.100 --> 00:12:12.480
Or was it a process of you learning about Bitcoin as well?

147
00:12:12.680 --> 00:12:13.900
I have a little hierarchy.

148
00:12:14.140 --> 00:12:17.000
I think everybody's born a skeptic.

149
00:12:17.320 --> 00:12:19.620
And so after the first hour, you're a skeptic.

150
00:12:19.640 --> 00:12:21.460
And you think maybe it's too good to be true.

151
00:12:22.240 --> 00:12:24.200
Or the government will ban it.

152
00:12:24.240 --> 00:12:25.320
Or it will get hacked.

153
00:12:25.640 --> 00:12:26.680
Or it's a gimmick.

154
00:12:27.380 --> 00:12:32.140
And then after 10 hours, you start to believe, oh, it's an asset.

155
00:12:33.100 --> 00:12:34.840
Maybe it's a good asset or a bad asset.

156
00:12:34.840 --> 00:12:38.600
But then you get upgraded from kind of skeptic to a trader.

157
00:12:39.540 --> 00:12:40.000
And you'll trade.

158
00:12:40.100 --> 00:12:41.460
Like, if it's cheap, I'll buy it.

159
00:12:41.500 --> 00:12:42.860
And if it's expensive, I'll sell it.

160
00:12:42.900 --> 00:12:43.860
But I'm just going to trade it.

161
00:12:43.940 --> 00:12:49.380
It's something like soybeans or like oil or like bushels of corn.

162
00:12:49.680 --> 00:12:53.320
At the $100 point, you think you become an investor.

163
00:12:53.660 --> 00:12:56.360
At that point, you think, wow, this is like Facebook for money.

164
00:12:56.360 --> 00:13:00.080
Like a bank in cyberspace where people can put their money.

165
00:13:00.760 --> 00:13:06.760
Or I can send money from New York to Tokyo without an intermediary.

166
00:13:07.060 --> 00:13:09.980
Or I can put money there and leave it there for 30 years.

167
00:13:10.220 --> 00:13:11.900
You know, I made a lot of money on Apple.

168
00:13:11.900 --> 00:13:13.460
Or I made a lot of money on Amazon.

169
00:13:13.460 --> 00:13:16.060
Or if you made money on Microsoft or Google.

170
00:13:16.560 --> 00:13:21.180
And if I said, hey, this is the big tech digital monetary network.

171
00:13:22.140 --> 00:13:23.620
It's just another big tech investment.

172
00:13:24.080 --> 00:13:26.940
After about $100, you're like, okay, well, that sounds like a good idea.

173
00:13:28.220 --> 00:13:34.020
My belief is, I mean, the way I made money in big tech investments is I find something

174
00:13:34.020 --> 00:13:36.600
everybody needs, nobody can stop.

175
00:13:36.820 --> 00:13:38.400
And most people don't understand.

176
00:13:38.660 --> 00:13:42.460
If you bought Amazon stock in 2010, it was everybody needs it.

177
00:13:42.500 --> 00:13:43.360
Nobody can stop it.

178
00:13:43.420 --> 00:13:44.580
But everybody disagreed with you.

179
00:13:45.000 --> 00:13:49.420
If you bought Amazon stock in 2020, everybody needed it.

180
00:13:49.420 --> 00:13:50.720
Everybody understood it.

181
00:13:50.780 --> 00:13:52.140
And everybody agreed with you.

182
00:13:52.380 --> 00:13:53.480
You see the difference?

183
00:13:53.600 --> 00:13:57.440
You don't want to buy it when everybody understands it and everybody agrees with you.

184
00:13:57.580 --> 00:14:01.800
You want to buy Apple in 2011 when everybody's going to need it.

185
00:14:01.840 --> 00:14:03.240
And nobody can stop it.

186
00:14:03.500 --> 00:14:06.540
But most people think the iPhone's only going to be worth $60.

187
00:14:07.100 --> 00:14:09.320
So that's the investor idea.

188
00:14:09.400 --> 00:14:10.500
You get there about 100 hours.

189
00:14:10.960 --> 00:14:15.100
And then in 1,000 hours, we say you become a maximalist.

190
00:14:15.100 --> 00:14:19.840
At that point, you say, wait a minute, this is, it's not a company.

191
00:14:20.660 --> 00:14:24.540
It's better than a big tech investment because it's not a security.

192
00:14:24.760 --> 00:14:26.280
You're not supporting Apple or Google.

193
00:14:26.480 --> 00:14:29.740
You're providing property rights to 8 billion people.

194
00:14:29.920 --> 00:14:37.660
You're providing freedom, integrity, property rights to 300 million companies, every country,

195
00:14:38.200 --> 00:14:39.420
every person on the planet.

196
00:14:39.420 --> 00:14:47.160
You know, rich people in New York own buildings, but taxi cab drivers in Nigeria don't own buildings

197
00:14:47.160 --> 00:14:47.980
in New York City.

198
00:14:48.240 --> 00:14:55.100
If I had $137 in Nigeria, I can't even buy $137 worth of a New York building.

199
00:14:55.360 --> 00:15:01.120
So you can see the rich New Yorkers have an advantage over the working class in Africa.

200
00:15:01.120 --> 00:15:08.860
But with Bitcoin, if you buy $237 of Bitcoin in Nigeria, you have the same property rights

201
00:15:08.860 --> 00:15:13.000
as a billionaire in New York or London that buys a billion of Bitcoin.

202
00:15:13.180 --> 00:15:14.200
You're para-pursue.

203
00:15:15.180 --> 00:15:17.240
You're not a limited partner.

204
00:15:17.600 --> 00:15:18.720
You don't own a security.

205
00:15:19.200 --> 00:15:20.760
You own the actual property.

206
00:15:21.240 --> 00:15:26.640
If you went and you bought $10 million worth of real estate in Zambia, Central Africa,

207
00:15:26.640 --> 00:15:32.340
there's nothing that I'm going to do in the United States that's going to increase the

208
00:15:32.340 --> 00:15:35.400
value of your property in Zambia, Central Africa.

209
00:15:36.260 --> 00:15:42.100
But on the other hand, if you bought $10 million of Bitcoin in Zambia, Central Africa, then somebody

210
00:15:42.100 --> 00:15:48.140
in Singapore with a Bloomberg terminal who's trading $20 billion a weekend is driving up

211
00:15:48.140 --> 00:15:51.340
the value of your property in Zambia, Central Africa.

212
00:15:51.340 --> 00:15:57.180
So when you join the Bitcoin network, you're getting the highest common denominator.

213
00:15:57.640 --> 00:16:03.720
The smartest, richest person anywhere in the world is working to make you wealthier.

214
00:16:04.340 --> 00:16:10.640
And when you buy a building, a warehouse in Alabama, or when you buy a building in rural

215
00:16:10.640 --> 00:16:14.080
China, that's the lowest common denominator.

216
00:16:14.620 --> 00:16:20.760
There's 0.01% of the people in rural China that might do something that might make your

217
00:16:20.760 --> 00:16:22.800
building more valuable, but nobody else cares.

218
00:16:23.580 --> 00:16:29.240
And so when you get to a thousand hours, you think, wait a minute, this is an ethical imperative,

219
00:16:29.780 --> 00:16:30.000
right?

220
00:16:30.020 --> 00:16:35.620
This is an instrument of economic empowerment for everyone on earth, every company on earth,

221
00:16:36.500 --> 00:16:40.900
every country on earth, every institution on earth, every family on earth.

222
00:16:40.900 --> 00:16:46.760
It is really a singularity because it's the first time in human history where you can convey

223
00:16:46.760 --> 00:16:53.280
actual property rights to an individual that cannot be taken from them via force.

224
00:16:53.980 --> 00:16:56.040
And I think that's a profound idea.

225
00:16:56.040 --> 00:16:57.480
I love what you said.

226
00:16:57.620 --> 00:17:02.660
