00:32:12.720 --> 00:32:13.240
No.

460
00:32:13.460 --> 00:32:15.120
Because when it drops, it drops a lot.

461
00:32:16.340 --> 00:32:16.620
Well, yeah.

462
00:32:16.860 --> 00:32:25.100
You know, actually, the fact that it's, first of all, I know there's a difference between volatility and risk.

463
00:32:25.100 --> 00:32:31.720
Volatility is when things oscillate or when they move around a lot.

464
00:32:31.880 --> 00:32:34.860
Risk is when you expect it to go to zero, right?

465
00:32:35.280 --> 00:32:37.740
So Bitcoin is volatile.

466
00:32:38.260 --> 00:32:40.080
And it's volatile because it's useful.

467
00:32:40.080 --> 00:32:53.000
On Saturday night, if you wanted to sell a billion dollars of something with 10 times leverage, you could post 100 million of collateral, sell a billion of Bitcoin, and we would give you the billion.

468
00:32:53.000 --> 00:32:59.760
And then on Sunday morning, if you wanted to reverse the trade and buy back the billion, you could do that as well.

469
00:33:00.460 --> 00:33:04.980
And you could do that with 100 million dollars of collateral, even 50 million of collateral.

470
00:33:05.160 --> 00:33:11.380
So Bitcoin, the network, is offering you extreme liquidity and extreme leverage at all times.

471
00:33:11.380 --> 00:33:16.680
If you think about the other assets like gold, if you wanted to sell a billion dollars of gold on Saturday night, you couldn't.

472
00:33:17.060 --> 00:33:22.580
If you wanted to sell a billion dollars of real estate in your hometown on Saturday night, you couldn't.

473
00:33:22.940 --> 00:33:27.540
If you wanted to borrow a billion dollars with 100 million of collateral, you couldn't.

474
00:33:28.060 --> 00:33:30.160
And so there isn't any leverage.

475
00:33:30.420 --> 00:33:31.820
And there's no credit.

476
00:33:31.840 --> 00:33:32.780
We'll call it credit.

477
00:33:32.920 --> 00:33:33.720
There's no credit.

478
00:33:34.300 --> 00:33:38.140
And there's no liquidity in all these other markets.

479
00:33:38.140 --> 00:33:42.380
I mean, so the reason they're not volatile is they're not useful, right?

480
00:33:42.480 --> 00:33:49.940
When they're panicked, when you think there's a missile crisis or a war or a disaster you want to sell, that's the only thing you can sell.

481
00:33:50.400 --> 00:33:54.600
And then when you're exuberant and you want to buy, it's the only thing you can buy.

482
00:33:54.600 --> 00:34:00.720
So that volatility is like the heat in the middle of a turbine in a jet engine.

483
00:34:00.980 --> 00:34:03.160
It's like, don't put your hand in the jet engine.

484
00:34:03.300 --> 00:34:04.180
That would be stupid.

485
00:34:04.180 --> 00:34:12.700
Am I afraid of the fact that the jet engine screams when it spins up and the fact that it blows, you know, hot air out the back?

486
00:34:12.760 --> 00:34:14.860
It's like, I'm not afraid of it.

487
00:34:14.940 --> 00:34:23.600
I just recognize if I want to fly from New York to Tokyo, I'm going to need a jet engine on the airplane because otherwise I'm not going to make it to my destination.

488
00:34:23.600 --> 00:34:33.780
And that volatility in Bitcoin, that is a manifestation of the energy, the financial energy in the system.

489
00:34:34.040 --> 00:34:36.380
And that's what drives superior performance.

490
00:34:36.600 --> 00:34:38.120
Well, that's a great analogy.

491
00:34:38.520 --> 00:34:40.240
OK, so please correct me if I'm wrong.

492
00:34:40.740 --> 00:34:46.920
Convertible bondholders, they can choose to convert their bonds into shares of MicroStrategy later on.

493
00:34:46.920 --> 00:34:54.140
What happens when MicroStrategy has to start paying back the principal when the bonds reach maturity?

494
00:34:54.140 --> 00:35:04.180
Well, for example, right now, the first bond we issued was struck at what would be about $39 a share, you know, in today's term.

495
00:35:04.240 --> 00:35:05.480
And we blew past that.

496
00:35:05.680 --> 00:35:10.660
And we eventually just converted to equity when it was 350% of that.

497
00:35:10.660 --> 00:35:16.380
So the people that bought that bond made billions and billions of dollars of capital gains and it became equity.

498
00:35:16.540 --> 00:35:23.220
All but like one of the bonds we've currently issued are already past their strike point.

499
00:35:23.360 --> 00:35:25.860
If they were to come due tomorrow, they would all just equitize.

500
00:35:26.480 --> 00:35:30.220
So for the most part, we expect that all the bonds will just convert to equity.

501
00:35:30.360 --> 00:35:31.760
We don't expect to ever pay them back.

502
00:35:31.760 --> 00:35:33.640
One bond was struck at $140.

503
00:35:33.940 --> 00:35:36.040
Another bond was struck at $149.

504
00:35:36.540 --> 00:35:38.780
A third bond is struck at $180.

505
00:35:39.220 --> 00:35:40.000
That is crazy.

506
00:35:40.000 --> 00:35:42.780
You know, a fourth bond is struck at $200.

507
00:35:43.060 --> 00:35:44.380
I think there's one at $240.

508
00:35:44.900 --> 00:35:53.120
So when the stock goes above $240, they're all pretty much equity instruments with varying degrees of downside protection.

509
00:35:53.520 --> 00:35:53.620
Right.

510
00:35:53.700 --> 00:35:59.220
And the truth is, the problem is not paying them back because they'll just equitize.

511
00:35:59.340 --> 00:36:03.740
And the reason they'll equitize, Bonnie, is because Bitcoin has been going up 50% a year.

512
00:36:03.740 --> 00:36:09.600
If, you know, my long-term forecast is Bitcoin goes up 29% a year on average over 21 years.

513
00:36:10.260 --> 00:36:12.380
But it's going up faster than that right now.

514
00:36:12.380 --> 00:36:16.000
If I'm bearish, I'm like, well, maybe Bitcoin will only go up 20% a year.

515
00:36:16.160 --> 00:36:20.740
If you have an asset going up 21% a year, you're doubling every three years.

516
00:36:20.980 --> 00:36:25.480
So the duration of that debt is like five years.

517
00:36:25.480 --> 00:36:28.520
Normally, we issue six-year bonds or something.

518
00:36:28.800 --> 00:36:37.320
So as long as the duration is like in the six-year time frame, normally, whatever you bought, you're going to double and double again.

519
00:36:37.860 --> 00:36:39.320
And it'll be volatile.

520
00:36:39.320 --> 00:36:44.020
But, you know, all the bonds are struck, you know, 40% premium.

521
00:36:44.520 --> 00:36:48.660
So normally, in 24 months, they're all equitizing.

522
00:36:48.660 --> 00:36:53.980
And we've got a tapered set of strikes and a tapered duration, ladder duration.

523
00:36:53.980 --> 00:37:00.840
So we can always refinance or roll forward or equitize something.

524
00:37:01.020 --> 00:37:03.500
So the problem is not paying off the bonds.

525
00:37:03.580 --> 00:37:06.980
The bonds are just selling equity forward at a premium.

526
00:37:06.980 --> 00:37:09.600
The problem is the delevering.

527
00:37:09.940 --> 00:37:13.140
The problem is when they all equitize, we don't have enough leverage.

528
00:37:13.560 --> 00:37:21.720
And we don't want to be not unlevered because if we're unlevered, then we start to approach the volatility of Bitcoin and the performance of Bitcoin.

529
00:37:22.360 --> 00:37:24.680
And our investors are Bitcoin maximalists.

530
00:37:24.680 --> 00:37:38.760
So what would you rather have, a real estate company that borrows $4 billion for 1% interest and has $1 billion in equity and has $5 billion in investments,

531
00:37:39.000 --> 00:37:46.620
or a real estate company that, you know, has $5 billion in equity and no leverage?

532
00:37:46.620 --> 00:37:55.960
You see, just about everybody would rather have the former rather than the latter because you're using someone else's free money in order to make an investment.

533
00:37:56.400 --> 00:38:02.880
If you think that real estate is going up more than 1% a year, then you want the former.

534
00:38:03.020 --> 00:38:06.540
If you think real estate's going down, you want the latter.

535
00:38:06.700 --> 00:38:13.420
But, of course, nobody invests in a company that doesn't believe in the underlying strategy of the company.

536
00:38:13.420 --> 00:38:26.200
So ultimately, what we'll do is we'll just continue to equitize those bonds as they come due and replace them with longer duration, more leveraged instruments of different types.

537
00:38:26.600 --> 00:38:30.260
And your ultimate goal is to increase Bitcoin per share.

538
00:38:30.460 --> 00:38:31.040
Is that correct?

539
00:38:31.320 --> 00:38:31.600
Yes.

540
00:38:31.820 --> 00:38:37.500
The number one goal of the company is to acquire more Bitcoin in an accretive fashion.

541
00:38:37.500 --> 00:38:43.140
An accretive meaning in a way that increases Bitcoin per share for our shareholders.

542
00:38:43.420 --> 00:38:46.960
You can acquire Bitcoin in a diluted fashion, but that would be stupid, right?

543
00:38:47.200 --> 00:38:54.580
So the way that we keep ourselves focused is we calculate the metric BTC yield.

544
00:38:55.480 --> 00:39:00.340
And BTC yield is the rate of increase in Bitcoin per share.

545
00:39:00.340 --> 00:39:19.460
So in a given year, if I say, well, we added 50,000 Bitcoin and we generated an 18% BTC yield, that says we got a lot of Bitcoin, but we also increased the Bitcoin per share by 18%.

546
00:39:19.460 --> 00:39:22.240
So you as a shareholder were not diluted.

547
00:39:22.680 --> 00:39:24.460
You were actually accreted.

548
00:39:24.560 --> 00:39:26.520
You were benefited through the operation.

549
00:39:27.520 --> 00:39:32.680
If I were to say, hey, we added a bunch of Bitcoin, but the BTC yield was minus 10%.

550
00:39:32.680 --> 00:39:36.420
Well, that was we did stupid things to get the Bitcoin.

551
00:39:36.660 --> 00:39:40.860
And even though it looks like we're bigger, you're actually poorer.

552
00:39:41.020 --> 00:39:41.940
We diluted the shares.

553
00:39:42.080 --> 00:39:43.880
Every company has an objective to grow.

554
00:39:44.500 --> 00:39:49.480
And they have a metric assets under management or revenue or EBITDA.

555
00:39:50.200 --> 00:39:50.600
Okay.

556
00:39:50.820 --> 00:39:53.680
But it's really the earnings per share, right?

557
00:39:53.680 --> 00:39:56.720
It's the metric per share that matters.

558
00:39:57.020 --> 00:39:59.360
If you're diluting, then you're doing the wrong thing.

559
00:39:59.540 --> 00:40:03.980
So the beauty for us is BTC yield is a very simple metric.

560
00:40:04.280 --> 00:40:10.560
It's just our Bitcoin divided by our fully diluted share count, the rate of change of that.

561
00:40:10.680 --> 00:40:13.340
And we report that when we do major deals.

562
00:40:13.640 --> 00:40:18.080
Like, you know, we do a billion dollar deal and we'll report what was the yield on that.

563
00:40:18.240 --> 00:40:20.080
And we report at the end of each quarter.

564
00:40:20.080 --> 00:40:26.700
And we also provide forward guidance or targets for our shareholders, you know, in the future.

565
00:40:26.820 --> 00:40:30.580
So they know that we're going to continue to generate positive yield for them.

566
00:40:30.940 --> 00:40:33.700
Very hard for general public to come to these numbers.

567
00:40:34.020 --> 00:40:35.060
And thank you for explaining.

568
00:40:35.340 --> 00:40:40.980
Earlier, you said Bitcoin will go up 21% for the next 21 years.

569
00:40:41.160 --> 00:40:45.580
How high do you think the price of Bitcoin could go in the mid to long term?

570
00:40:45.580 --> 00:40:55.200
So let's say how many Bitcoin should someone aim to accumulate to live comfortably in the next 10 years, 20 years, 30 years?

571
00:40:56.180 --> 00:41:02.100
Well, my target, my base case, right?

572
00:41:02.160 --> 00:41:09.600
My expectation is Bitcoin will continue to grow as digital capital.

573
00:41:09.600 --> 00:41:12.960
It'll serve as a long term store of value for the world.

574
00:41:13.540 --> 00:41:16.680
Right now, it's 0.1% of the world's assets.

575
00:41:16.860 --> 00:41:21.380
It's about $1.4 trillion out of $900 trillion.

576
00:41:21.920 --> 00:41:25.940
It's big in crypto economy, but it's fairly small in mainstream economy.

577
00:41:26.160 --> 00:41:34.380
I think the entire worldwide addressable market for store of value for long term capital is about $450 trillion.

578
00:41:34.380 --> 00:41:38.340
So 1.4 out of $450 trillion.

579
00:41:39.100 --> 00:41:47.240
And I expect Bitcoin will grow progressively to be 7% of the assets of the world, not 0.1%.

580
00:41:47.240 --> 00:41:52.840
And when it gets to 7%, it'll be 13 million a coin.

581
00:41:53.500 --> 00:41:56.580
So 13 million, and that's over 21 years.

582
00:41:56.720 --> 00:41:58.280
So by the year 2045.

583
00:41:58.280 --> 00:42:05.600
And I think right now it's been growing for the last four years, it's been up about 50% a year.

584
00:42:05.820 --> 00:42:18.260
And it's gradually decelerating its growth rate because as it gets bigger and as it gets more widely adopted and the law of large numbers kicks in, it should decelerate.

585
00:42:18.340 --> 00:42:24.860
If you look back over the last 100 years, the U.S. dollar money supply expands about 7% a year.

586
00:42:24.860 --> 00:42:30.540
So I'm just thinking it'll continue for the next 20 or 30 years at that trend.

587
00:42:31.380 --> 00:42:44.860
And once you crank in that number and some assumptions about inflation and technology, the conclusion is all the other asset classes will grow plus or minus a few percent.

588
00:42:45.400 --> 00:42:47.000
Equity will grow a bit faster.

589
00:42:47.360 --> 00:42:49.200
Real estate about the same.

590
00:42:49.360 --> 00:42:51.020
Perhaps gold slower.

591
00:42:51.020 --> 00:43:05.380
Bitcoin will grow faster because it is demonetizing gold, demonetizing real estate, demonetizing certain equity indexes, and demonetizing corporate bond portfolios.

592
00:43:05.720 --> 00:43:11.080
These are all things that wealthy people store their wealth in, in order to just keep it.

593
00:43:11.080 --> 00:43:29.040
And so if you see Bitcoin is going 50% to 45% growth to 40% growth to 35% growth to 32% growth to 30% to 28% to 25%, at some point it's approaching the returns of the S&P index or equity indexes.

594
00:43:29.040 --> 00:43:33.020
And they've been like 12%, you know, 10% to 12%.

595
00:43:33.020 --> 00:43:34.880
And so Bitcoin is moving toward that.

596
00:43:35.040 --> 00:43:36.980
Those companies are going to buy Bitcoin.

597
00:43:37.080 --> 00:43:40.040
And as they buy Bitcoin, they're going to actually merge.

598
00:43:40.140 --> 00:43:52.900
And so these two lines of what we'll call traditional capital, which is diversified equity portfolios, and digital capital, which is what Bitcoin is, they're moving toward each other.

599
00:43:52.900 --> 00:43:58.900
The volatility of conventional corporations is the VIX.

600
00:43:59.040 --> 00:44:01.560
If you study the VIX, and it's like 15, 16.

601
00:44:02.140 --> 00:44:05.220
And the volatility of Bitcoin is what we call devol.

602
00:44:05.520 --> 00:44:06.760
And you can, it's like 55.

603
00:44:07.960 --> 00:44:10.080
The devol is moving toward the VIX.

604
00:44:10.080 --> 00:44:21.940
And when Bitcoin is a $200 trillion asset class, you know, maybe the Bitcoin volatility is 20 and the VIX is 15.

605
00:44:22.920 --> 00:44:25.040
Bitcoin is always going to be a bit more volatile.

606
00:44:25.460 --> 00:44:32.720
It's always going to be performing a bit more because it's global, open 24-7, pure digital capital.

607
00:44:32.720 --> 00:44:42.000
And it strips away the risk factors of companies, corporations, and it strips away the risk factors of property, right, of real estate.

608
00:44:42.640 --> 00:44:44.380
And so that's why it's going to outperform.

609
00:44:44.960 --> 00:44:50.440
And I've explained why it's more volatile because it's always going to be more tradable, more global.

610
00:44:51.160 --> 00:44:55.360
Some guy can short it with 100x leverage in Singapore at midnight.

611
00:44:55.560 --> 00:44:58.280
That's illegal in New York to do with equity.

612
00:44:58.280 --> 00:45:03.420
So the point is there are some things you can do with Bitcoin you'll never do with traditional assets.

613
00:45:04.320 --> 00:45:08.300
But when you crank all that in, and I did it in a model.

614
00:45:08.420 --> 00:45:10.100
It's called the Bitcoin 24 model.

615
00:45:10.300 --> 00:45:11.960
You can Google Bitcoin 24.

616
00:45:12.720 --> 00:45:15.120
And if you Google it, you'll find a link to GitHub.

617
00:45:15.380 --> 00:45:18.020
And you can go download the Bitcoin 24 model.

618
00:45:18.140 --> 00:45:24.360
And you can put in all your own assumptions for all these macroeconomic factors and all these technical factors.

619
00:45:24.360 --> 00:45:29.620
And you can run your own micro models for your family and your corporation and your country.

620
00:45:30.280 --> 00:45:32.260
So, you know, don't take my word for it.

621
00:45:32.280 --> 00:45:32.980
Try it out yourself.

622
00:45:33.160 --> 00:45:41.540
But if you download Bitcoin 24 or you look at my assumptions, my assumptions are basically, you know, those things.

623
00:45:41.700 --> 00:45:48.360
And the result that pops out is Bitcoin, you know, on average over the 21 years has an annualized rate of return of 29%.

624
00:45:48.360 --> 00:45:56.540
And that 29% just, it grows with volatility up to 13 million of Bitcoin.

625
00:45:56.740 --> 00:46:04.280
So you can, if you wanted to figure out how much will you have in 10 years, you could download the model and put in your assumptions and look at the 10-year point.

626
00:46:04.280 --> 00:46:06.080
Maybe you're more bearish.

627
00:46:06.260 --> 00:46:08.600
The bear case would be only 3 million a coin.

628
00:46:08.700 --> 00:46:11.120
And the bull case is 49 million a coin.

629
00:46:11.360 --> 00:46:13.060
Everyone can have their own assumptions.

630
00:46:13.060 --> 00:46:26.180
But, you know, I think if you want something simple, if you want to reduce their simple sentence, I would say if you're a young person, every Bitcoin you buy today is probably worth $13 million in U.S. dollars in 21 years.

631
00:46:26.600 --> 00:46:29.760
So if you can get 5 Bitcoin, you would have 65 million.

632
00:46:30.280 --> 00:46:33.820
Now, it won't buy as much in 21 years as it buys today.

633
00:46:33.960 --> 00:46:36.000
But it'll buy a lot.

634
00:46:36.820 --> 00:46:38.920
It'll buy, you know, it'll buy a lot.

635
00:46:38.920 --> 00:46:46.340
I mean, consumer products will appreciate it 2% or 3% a year or they'll get cheaper because of technology.

636
00:46:46.680 --> 00:47:00.080
And on the other hand, Palm Beach real estate, if you want a beach house in the Hamptons or Palm Beach or you want the best condo in the middle of the biggest city, that's going to go up 7%, you know, 6%, 7%.

637
00:47:00.080 --> 00:47:07.460
So, you know, you kind of you want to beat the 7% if you want to buy rich things, expensive things.

638
00:47:08.020 --> 00:47:11.780
You want to beat the 2% if you want to buy the cheap things.

639
00:47:12.260 --> 00:47:14.380
And I think Bitcoin kind of beats everything.

640
00:47:14.620 --> 00:47:16.360
So that's why it's a pretty good idea.

641
00:47:16.780 --> 00:47:22.220
Do you think Bitcoin is to keep the rich rich or to empower the poor?

642
00:47:22.220 --> 00:47:29.240
Because you see a lot of stories of, you know, people with really low paying jobs and they came across Bitcoin.

643
00:47:29.360 --> 00:47:33.100
All of a sudden, they're this, you know, really rich kid living in Dubai.

644
00:47:34.360 --> 00:47:36.300
And people love these stories.

645
00:47:37.300 --> 00:47:40.920
I think Bitcoin powers the future.

646
00:47:40.920 --> 00:47:54.520
So the answer is it's going to empower the 8 billion people on the planet and the working class to a greater degree than any other financial asset ever invented.

647
00:47:54.880 --> 00:48:04.040
There's no other asset that you can put on an Android phone and buy $40 a week worth of that gives you the same property rights as a mega billionaire.

648
00:48:04.040 --> 00:48:12.100
You have better property rights than the richest person on earth, than a Jeff Bezos or than an Elon Musk or Bill Gates.

649
00:48:12.360 --> 00:48:15.400
You have better performance than they have.

650
00:48:16.260 --> 00:48:21.680
And you can buy and sell it and it's liquid to you at all times.

651
00:48:21.960 --> 00:48:24.940
So, you know, there's nothing better than that.

652
00:48:24.940 --> 00:48:38.060
And on the other hand, the wealthy people that reject empowerment for everybody else, that they reject the crypto economy and they reject Bitcoin, they're going to get poor, right?

653
00:48:38.060 --> 00:48:40.820
They're going to be invested in 7, 8, 9 percent.

654
00:48:41.100 --> 00:48:44.520
The really conservative ones will get 7 percent return.

655
00:48:44.920 --> 00:48:48.600
The normal ones will get 10 to 12 percent return.

656
00:48:49.280 --> 00:48:51.880
The people that buy into Bitcoin are 29 percent, right?

657
00:48:51.880 --> 00:48:58.860
So the rich will probably still say rich, but they will lose position.

658
00:48:59.360 --> 00:49:14.060
The smart money, the wealthy people that embrace the future, that embrace the digital economy, they'll get wealthier, but they will also boost the working class and the poor that get on that train with them, right?

659
00:49:14.100 --> 00:49:17.080
They're basically all working together, right?

660
00:49:17.160 --> 00:49:18.260
In a coalition.

661
00:49:18.260 --> 00:49:26.820
So I think Bitcoin is going to drive the digital transformation of the capital markets.

662
00:49:27.640 --> 00:49:35.000
And that means that, you know, everybody that buys any security that's backed by Bitcoin is going to get better yield.

663
00:49:35.200 --> 00:49:36.560
They're going to get better upside.

664
00:49:36.700 --> 00:49:37.860
They're going to get better performance.

665
00:49:37.860 --> 00:49:43.740
So indirectly, Bitcoin benefits pension funds, retirees.

666
00:49:44.440 --> 00:49:58.960
I think, you know, people that hold stock or exposure to Bitcoin or micro strategy, 250 million people have something right now that's exposed directly to Bitcoin.

667
00:49:58.960 --> 00:50:01.340
And that will eventually spread to a billion.

668
00:50:01.340 --> 00:50:05.000
So everybody in the world benefits indirectly.

669
00:50:06.140 --> 00:50:11.100
It's like you benefit indirectly by owning any asset that has Bitcoin exposure.

